        "questionText": "What are like terms?",
        "options": [],
        "correctAnswer": "Terms that have exactly the same variables raised to the same powers.",
        "explanation": "Only like terms can be added or subtracted, for example 3x + 5x = 8x.",
        "difficulty": "easy"
      },
      {
        "questionText": "What does it mean to expand an algebraic expression?",
        "options": [],
        "correctAnswer": "To remove brackets by multiplying each term inside by the term outside.",
        "explanation": "For example a(b + c) = ab + ac.",
        "difficulty": "easy"
      },
      {
        "questionText": "What is factorization?",
        "options": [],
        "correctAnswer": "Writing an expression as a product of its factors.",
        "explanation": "It is the reverse of expansion, for example 6x + 9 = 3(2x + 3).",
        "difficulty": "easy"
      },
      {
        "questionText": "What is the common factor method of factorization?",
        "options": [],
        "correctAnswer": "Taking out the highest common factor of all terms outside a bracket.",
        "explanation": "For example 4x + 8 = 4(x + 2).",
        "difficulty": "easy"
      },
      {
        "questionText": "State the difference of two squares formula.",
        "options": [],
        "correctAnswer": "a^2 - b^2 = (a + b)(a - b).",
        "explanation": "It applies only when two perfect squares are subtracted.",
        "difficulty": "easy"
      },
      {
        "questionText": "How do you factorize a quadratic trinomial x^2 + bx + c?",
